\chapter{Checked-HIR optimization and candidate validation}
\label{ch:74}
\index{Checked-HIR optimization and candidate validation}

Checked HIR is the common high-level input for direct C11 and source-to-MIR lowering. Optimization selects enabled passes over checked data and validates transformed candidates before publishing them. A rewrite can be type-correct yet unsound if it changes an effect, checks a trap in a different order, retains fewer references, or loses an active loan. The rewrite subsystem must preserve more than the nominal result type.

\section{Compare an identity rule}
Use \OnxCodeTerm{--list-optimizations} to record the active rules. Compare default output with \OnxCodeTerm{--no-optimizations --enable-opt integer\_add\_zero\_right} on a fixture containing that expression. An effectful or trapping operand on the left still needs evaluation. Test a simple constant input, an effectful use within the admitted profile, and a boundary input. If a rule is unavailable, check the selected compiler configuration and profile. MIR block-level optimization runs later and uses a different verifier and work budget.

\section{Compare two Boolean evaluation traces}
Let two functions print their own names and return Boolean values. Consider
negating both calls and comparing the results for equality. The authored
canonicalization can compare the original operands directly: complementing
both bits does not change whether they agree. If the left call prints L
and returns false, and the right prints R and returns true, the trace is
L, R, false in both versions. If the left call traps after printing L,
there is no R. Equality is eager but ordered; replacing it with a lazy
test on the left value would satisfy some truth-table rows while losing
the second print.

Why recheck the candidate when the Boolean identity is valid? The rewrite
must move each captured operand with its original lexical reference,
type and location. A duplicated left subtree in the right-hand position
could still have Boolean type; a newly constructed reference could also
name an out-of-scope local. The checker can reject malformed references,
while the evaluation trace distinguishes two well-typed programs with
different effects. These checks answer complementary questions.

In a unit test, keep the checked input available, let one internal
rewrite succeed, then exhaust the processor's rewrite budget before it
finishes. Inspect the caller's tree, not just the reported limit. The
caller must see the original checked unit, not a half-normalized expression
with one negation removed. Only the private candidate was changed. This
transaction boundary makes the failure test informative even without an
emitted executable.

